1
00:00:01,120 --> 00:00:08,720
Welcome to the Subclub podcast, a show dedicated to the best practices for building and growing app businesses.

2
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:19,360
We sit down with the entrepreneurs, investors and builders behind the most successful apps in the world to learn from their successes and failures.

3
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:22,480
Subclub is brought to you by RevenueCat.

4
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:32,720
Thousands of the world's best apps trust RevenueCat to power in app purchases, manage customers, and grow revenue across iOS, Android, and the web.

5
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:38,240
You can learn more at revenuecat dot com dot Let's get into the show.

6
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:46,000
Hello, I'm your host, David Barnard, and with me today, RevenueCat CEO, Jacob Biding.

7
00:00:47,040 --> 00:00:51,840
Our guest today is Jason Vandemerva, Director of Growth Engineering at Strava.

8
00:00:52,560 --> 00:01:01,360
Jason helped found the Growth team and has been integral in evolving it to where it is today, with five cross-functional teams totaling over seventy people.

9
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:14,560
On the podcast, we talk with Jason about some of Strava's big growth wins, the importance of feature education, and whether or not all product teams should actually be growth teams.

10
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:18,720
Hey, Jason, thank you so much for joining us today on the podcast.

11
00:01:19,360 --> 00:01:20,080
It's great to be here.

12
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:21,120
Thanks for inviting me.

13
00:01:21,760 --> 00:01:23,840
And Jacob, always nice to chat with you.

14
00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:24,960
Let's talk about apps.

15
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:26,080
I'm here to talk about apps.

16
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:32,160
I'm here to talk about crushing KOMs as it is for the Strava heads out there.

17
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:33,680
All right.

18
00:01:34,080 --> 00:01:39,520
So, Jason, I wanted to ask you about your role as a growth engineer.

19
00:01:40,160 --> 00:01:53,120
You know, I think a lot of people who don't work at bigger companies probably don't really fully understand what a role like that looks like, especially at an app company, because, you know, how many app companies are there now with growth engineersers and growth engineering teams.

20
00:01:53,760 --> 00:01:57,280
So tell me a little bit about your role as a growth engineer.

21
00:01:58,080 --> 00:02:02,640
I think a lot of it comes down to the goal and the mindset of the work you're doing.

22
00:02:03,280 --> 00:02:10,560
I always say growth teams, their goal is to connect users with the value of your product and to continue to expand that value.

23
00:02:11,200 --> 00:02:21,040
And so that requires an orientation on user and business impact at the end of the day versus perhaps just delivering something you were told to deliver.

24
00:02:21,520 --> 00:02:27,760
And I think that orientation is really important because in order to understand and get to that impact, you have to do a lot of learning.

25
00:02:29,040 --> 00:02:30,720
comes in all different shapes and sizes, right?

26
00:02:30,880 --> 00:02:49,600
There's like the small optimization, like how can we improve this flow or reduce friction to this flow or how do we build something massive to really change the game for our business, but you're building something massive not because you want to build something for the sake of it, but because there's a goal in mind, kind of a business and user outcome in mind.

27
00:02:49,920 --> 00:02:56,640
And so it's really, it's a little bit of a mindset shift and a willingness to sometimes go faster than is comfortable within engineering.

28
00:02:56,880 --> 00:03:06,560
Sometimes you do things that are super hacky in the short term so that you get that learning to determine if you do the bigger investment and a willingness to kind of almost break the rules at times.

29
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:12,240
Growth engineers are not the best engineers to run your kind of infrastructure teams.

30
00:03:13,280 --> 00:03:14,240
So it's a little bit different.

31
00:03:14,560 --> 00:03:20,320
And I think engineers who are very user focused are great growth engineers.

32
00:03:20,560 --> 00:03:24,880
Engineers who think of themselves as maybe I want to be a product manager some day is really great.

33
00:03:25,120 --> 00:03:31,200
So I often say I want our engineers to think like product managers, thinking about that user problem they're trying to solve.

34
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:33,040
Yeah, that's fantastic.

35
00:03:33,520 --> 00:03:39,280
So you've been at Strava for almost eight years and Strava is almost sixteen years old.

36
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:53,440
So you've been there almost half the life of the company., which is really cool because there's a lot of kind of water under the bridge before you joined, but then you've had a lot of impact and seen a lot of change as the app has grown and the company has grown over the years.

37
00:03:54,240 --> 00:04:02,640
So I'm curious what marketing was like before and growth was like at Stravo as you joined.

38
00:04:03,200 --> 00:04:06,960
And then I know you were kind of foundational in forming the growth team.

39
00:04:07,200 --> 00:04:09,760
So I'll want to talk a little bit more about that as well.

40
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:11,440
Yeah, it's a great question.

41
00:04:11,840 --> 00:04:17,920
I mean, and Jacob, you kind of alluded to this at the beginning when you talked about KOMs and you referenced our brand.

42
00:04:18,240 --> 00:04:26,320
And I think that's one of the coolest parts about Strava is we've had we've become part of the zeitgeist for first cyclists and then runners.

43
00:04:27,280 --> 00:04:34,400
And now we're trying to broaden to the zeitgeist of everyone who's trying to be active, regardless of your sport.

44
00:04:35,040 --> 00:04:42,320
And so when we got to Strava, really, you know, we were really big in the cycling community and then the running piece we were picking up a lot of steam on.

45
00:04:42,960 --> 00:04:47,760
And so then and even now, most of Strava's growth is word of mouth.

46
00:04:48,480 --> 00:04:49,520
People love Strava.

47
00:04:49,680 --> 00:04:53,600
There's a phrase that the community uses that if it's not on Strava, it didn't happen.

48
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:55,360
And that's not something we came up with.

49
00:04:55,600 --> 00:04:56,080
They came up with that.

50
00:04:56,560 --> 00:04:59,040
And so that's amazing, right?

51
00:04:59,280 --> 00:05:06,000
It's an amazing position to be in where your growth is word of mouth and organic and you're not having to depend on paid dollars for growth.

52
00:05:06,320 --> 00:05:09,760
Obviously, we do those aspects, but the organic piece is huge.

53
00:05:09,920 --> 00:05:12,640
So then you're thinking, okay, what's the point of a growth team?

54
00:05:13,280 --> 00:05:21,920
And a lot of it is looking at our experiences, looking at our funnels and reducing friction and finding new, novel ways to cancel more growth.

55
00:05:22,400 --> 00:05:27,280
You know, when we started, I joined Strava and two weeks later they were like, hey, we're going to make a growth team.

56
00:05:27,920 --> 00:05:28,880
We have no idea what this means.

57
00:05:29,040 --> 00:05:29,520
Oh, wow.

58
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:31,040
So eight years ago, let me do the math.

59
00:05:31,040 --> 00:05:33,600
That's like, what, what, what, twenty two, twenty five?

60
00:05:33,680 --> 00:05:34,720
2015, something like that.

61
00:05:34,800 --> 00:05:35,440
2015, yeah.

62
00:05:35,760 --> 00:05:36,000
Around there.

63
00:05:36,160 --> 00:05:37,120
Yeah, so early.

64
00:05:37,600 --> 00:05:39,920
Just to center me, like when were you guys on the App Store?

65
00:05:40,400 --> 00:05:42,400
Because sixteen years ago precedes the App Store.

66
00:05:42,560 --> 00:05:44,640
And then what was the product before the App Store?

67
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:45,760
There was a before the App Store.

68
00:05:46,640 --> 00:05:47,440
There was a web app, yeah.

69
00:05:47,680 --> 00:05:48,800
So, a web app, interesting.

70
00:05:49,440 --> 00:05:57,600
The product started with kind of this insight from some of our founders of our two main co founders, Mark and Michael, were Harvard Rovers together.

71
00:05:57,840 --> 00:06:04,160
They'd run a business together and they were like, they wanted to recreate the locker room camaraderie digitally, right?

72
00:06:04,480 --> 00:06:09,840
So they had this idea and then they met this guy named Davey who had, was starting to buy the Garmin devices.

73
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:14,480
And he was trying all these crazy things with this Garmin device.

74
00:06:14,640 --> 00:06:16,080
And it was like, okay, what do we do with this data?

75
00:06:16,240 --> 00:06:17,440
Like, this data is so cool.

76
00:06:18,240 --> 00:06:19,520
And then there was web app.

77
00:06:19,760 --> 00:06:23,520
And so the way it started was they would take a van and they'd go to local cycling races.

78
00:06:24,240 --> 00:06:31,760
And then they would get all the cyclists to come over and upload, literally plug in their Garmin devices and they would upload to Strava for them.

79
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:36,560
Oh, I guess that was, yeah, because that was right when bike computers were becoming like an accessible thing.

80
00:06:36,720 --> 00:06:40,800
That was probably the only real personal GPS device that was mass market at that point.

81
00:06:41,120 --> 00:06:41,360
Exactly.

82
00:06:41,440 --> 00:06:42,960
Because phones didn't have it yet, right?

83
00:06:43,200 --> 00:06:43,280
No.

84
00:06:43,520 --> 00:06:46,080
And so I, our app started in 2011.

85
00:06:46,320 --> 00:06:51,840
So a little bit after, you know, the, the, the, the, the rise of the, the iPhone and the App Store.

86
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:55,200
Well, but the iPhone didn't have great, like, location ability before that.

87
00:06:55,360 --> 00:06:59,520
It was all sky hook and like sub, sub GPS until maybe 2010, 2011.

88
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:00,320
So it makes sense.

89
00:07:00,400 --> 00:07:00,560
Right.

90
00:07:00,720 --> 00:07:07,680
And it wasn't a great experience anyway, but it was, and, and there's also the cultural piece of, don't want to carry a phone when I'm running.

91
00:07:08,080 --> 00:07:15,120
You know, nowadays we don't, people for the most part don't seem to care, but there was kind of this interesting, I'm not going to carry my phone with me when I, when I run.

92
00:07:15,760 --> 00:07:17,440
So 2011 in the App Store.

93
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:20,560
Um, so yeah, we were in the App Store for a few years when I joined.

94
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:25,360
And I, when I joined actually, we had, we had just combined our run app and our ride app.

95
00:07:25,680 --> 00:07:29,520
So when we expanded into running, we made a Strava run app right.

96
00:07:29,760 --> 00:07:32,560
The day there was Nike run, there's still Nike run, but there's Nike run.

97
00:07:33,120 --> 00:07:34,160
Fantastic run keeper.

98
00:07:34,480 --> 00:07:35,520
So we made the run app.

99
00:07:36,160 --> 00:07:41,600
I think that helped a lot with the communication to runners and the broading to runners.

100
00:07:42,320 --> 00:07:44,080
So, so again, back to the question though.

101
00:07:44,400 --> 00:07:51,680
Um, was there a marketing team and is there still kind of a separate marketing team that's completely separate from the growth team?

102
00:07:52,560 --> 00:07:52,640
Yeah.

103
00:07:52,880 --> 00:07:53,280
Great question.

104
00:07:53,680 --> 00:08:06,160
So back then there was, um, there was a marketing team that was kind of, you know, all kinds of marketing, but kind of your classic grow local communities, go to events, develop relationships with professionals.

105
00:08:06,320 --> 00:08:14,720
I think that's one of the things that makes Strava so unique is, you know, every, you know, the major running and cycling professionals like Chris Froome or Molly Sydal are on Strava.

106
00:08:15,040 --> 00:08:17,440
You can go, you can go to compare your wattage with theirs.

107
00:08:17,600 --> 00:08:18,240
It's amazing.

108
00:08:18,400 --> 00:08:21,680
And you can see their, see their, just, you know, obviously, we're not like them.

109
00:08:21,920 --> 00:08:25,360
Um, but it's really inspiring to see the reminder.

110
00:08:25,840 --> 00:08:26,000
Right.

111
00:08:26,880 --> 00:08:31,440
And it's a great way for them to, you know, connect in a very organic way with their fans.

112
00:08:31,680 --> 00:08:31,840
Right.

113
00:08:32,080 --> 00:08:43,600
Um, and so our marketing org has done a lot of that and just generally supporting the world of sport and helping people become more active.

114
00:08:44,080 --> 00:08:47,680
When we started the growth team, it was very cross functional and it's always been cross functional.

115
00:08:47,840 --> 00:08:52,640
So when we say we're a product team, we're the most broad definition of that possible.

116
00:08:52,880 --> 00:09:00,720
Like we have growth marketing functions where those ladder up to from a reporting perspective is kind of irrelevant.

117
00:09:01,200 --> 00:09:05,440
Our teams have always had growth marketers on the team, product marketers on the team.

118
00:09:05,920 --> 00:09:09,920
We've always seen ourselves as very horizontal versus vertical of a model.

119
00:09:10,960 --> 00:09:13,600
So it's interesting, I guess.

120
00:09:15,280 --> 00:09:25,600
You see this a lot with apps that break out like Strava that it doesn't necessarily follow the pattern we hear a lot in apps where it's like, you know, get a decent LTV CAC and try to scale that way.

121
00:09:25,840 --> 00:09:27,920
You have some for you, it's organic.

122
00:09:28,080 --> 00:09:39,920
And because you have organic things like, you know, taking a van and going to a race, kind of scale because you get five people on board that's going to turn into potentially, you know, five more, et cetera, et cetera.

123
00:09:40,800 --> 00:09:42,400
And that's a really good place to start.

124
00:09:42,560 --> 00:09:45,200
And that's always just downstream of having a good product.

125
00:09:45,520 --> 00:09:51,200
And so I'm curious, like, how much of like the growth team's work?

126
00:09:51,680 --> 00:09:56,160
Because I imagine as a growth team, you're touching, you know, top to bottom, sort of everything.

127
00:09:57,200 --> 00:10:09,040
Like, how does that influence in the, like, the road map, you know, from when you started to, to, because obviously there's like product folks who want to build, oh, we're talking to customers and they want X.

128
00:10:09,360 --> 00:10:13,280
And then there's like growth, which is, yeah, the customer is important.

129
00:10:13,840 --> 00:10:21,360
Uh, you obviously if you're not building stuff, they want, but then there's also this goal of, you know, increasing adoption and things like this.

130
00:10:21,680 --> 00:10:24,080
So how do you guys, how do you guys work that tension?

131
00:10:24,240 --> 00:10:30,320
And was there, was there resistance, uh, when you came in to be like, oh, we're going to be a team that's focused on numbers, you know?

132
00:10:30,880 --> 00:10:30,960
Yeah.

133
00:10:31,280 --> 00:10:33,680
It was definitely, it was definitely hard at the beginning.

134
00:10:33,760 --> 00:10:37,120
I mean, we we kind of started this team and to be fair, we didn't know what we were doing.

135
00:10:37,520 --> 00:10:42,240
We were told, I think we were told, kind of, by the board, like, hey, you know, top companies have growth teams.

136
00:10:42,320 --> 00:10:42,960
Go figure it out.

137
00:10:43,280 --> 00:10:44,480
I read this in the RevFlight magazine.

138
00:10:44,640 --> 00:10:45,600
You guys needed growth teams.

139
00:10:45,760 --> 00:10:46,800
And we're okay, cool.

140
00:10:47,120 --> 00:10:53,120
So the first, the first experiment we ever ran was adding the ability to add your profile photo to the onboarding flow.

141
00:10:53,840 --> 00:10:54,400
I remember that.

142
00:10:54,960 --> 00:10:55,280
Classic.

143
00:10:55,440 --> 00:10:56,240
Classic growth hack.

144
00:10:56,640 --> 00:10:57,440
Classic 2015.

145
00:10:57,680 --> 00:11:01,680
You know, and then of course later on removed it because we realized like it adds too much friction, right?

146
00:11:02,320 --> 00:11:12,800
But we ran and I think a lot for people out there, like when you're starting growth teams., don't over index on the impact slash importance of specific tests.

147
00:11:13,280 --> 00:11:20,240
Like there are times in the growth journey where we have new people joining or a new team joining where the first few months, I'm like, just run AB tests.

148
00:11:20,560 --> 00:11:25,840
Like figure out as a team how to go from idea, hypothesis, running the test, evaluation, determining.

149
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:32,160
Like it's okay if you didn't knock the ball out of the park or the metric you were pushing for wasn't that important.

150
00:11:32,560 --> 00:11:37,760
What matters more is you have a metric you're going for, you're thinking about it, your brain's storming, you know, that piece.

151
00:11:37,920 --> 00:11:40,240
So at the beginning we were definitely kind of like, what do we do?

152
00:11:40,480 --> 00:11:46,080
But one of the great pieces, um, I think that the beginning was the inclusion of user research on our team.

153
00:11:46,800 --> 00:11:55,360
And we would run, we didn't have a big team, so we would run tests using usertesting dot com and we would watch them as a team, small team.

154
00:11:56,080 --> 00:12:04,000
And one of the things we had looked at, our product analyst had found this insight of, you know, the most important thing for a new user is to upload.

155
00:12:04,640 --> 00:12:06,560
Yet we see a big drop off.

156
00:12:07,040 --> 00:12:08,720
We did a funnel analysis, big drop off.

157
00:12:08,960 --> 00:12:14,400
So we did a bunch of user testing to say, you know, give a bunch of people the ability to go through the onboarding flow.

158
00:12:14,640 --> 00:12:15,600
And we watched it as a team.

159
00:12:15,920 --> 00:12:17,040
Very important to do that.

160
00:12:17,200 --> 00:12:18,800
Everyone should be watching user interviews.

161
00:12:18,960 --> 00:12:22,800
And we saw people would get to the record screen and say, what is this?

162
00:12:23,280 --> 00:12:24,240
What am I supposed to tap?

163
00:12:24,880 --> 00:12:28,400
And the problem there was skeuromorphism was really big.

164
00:12:28,800 --> 00:12:38,800
And so all the buttons on our record screen, like where you like, you know, record your bike or run were, um, had taken homage from a real remote from a TV.

165
00:12:39,280 --> 00:12:43,280
So it had the record button was round with a little red button in it, right?

166
00:12:43,760 --> 00:12:45,840
And the stop button was literally like a square, right?

167
00:12:46,160 --> 00:12:50,160
Things that you would find on your DVD remote, but users didn't know what to do with that.

168
00:12:50,320 --> 00:12:51,600
So we were like, what are we going to do?

169
00:12:51,680 --> 00:12:53,120
We're going to put text on every button.

170
00:12:53,200 --> 00:12:54,800
We're going to make it so obvious how to use it.

171
00:12:55,040 --> 00:12:55,200
Oh, okay.

172
00:12:55,440 --> 00:12:55,520
Yeah.

173
00:12:55,680 --> 00:12:55,760
Yeah.

174
00:12:55,920 --> 00:12:56,400
That's the thing.

175
00:12:56,480 --> 00:13:07,120
I was like, well, I think round is still pretty usual, but if you don't have labels, I fought, you know, designer I've fought with over the years, because it's like, it's not going to hurt.

176
00:13:07,280 --> 00:13:10,240
I know it makes your design look bad, but it's necessary.

177
00:13:11,200 --> 00:13:11,360
Right.

178
00:13:11,520 --> 00:13:12,160
It's so funny.

179
00:13:12,400 --> 00:13:20,720
And we went back and forth on like, where do we, where do we put the text on the label buttons and how do you localize copy to go into a small button?

180
00:13:20,880 --> 00:13:27,440
It turns out you can say go, you know, geo in many languages that are in English and they still, you know, that works very well.

181
00:13:27,680 --> 00:13:36,880
So we did that and that was one of the biggest wins we've ever had in reducing the friction to upload, increasing upload rate for new users and that results in a huge increase in retention for new users.

182
00:13:37,760 --> 00:13:44,080
And so since then we've kind of So a lot of our work has been kind of reducing friction to that first kind of upload moment.

183
00:13:44,320 --> 00:13:51,200
Yeah, because then after you've recorded your first thing, you've got data in Strava now and you you're starting it's building value as a repository.

184
00:13:51,440 --> 00:13:59,360
And I mean, I think that's a one of the, again, those things that create take a take an app and make it something that becomes sticky forever.

185
00:14:00,160 --> 00:14:03,360
It's building lock in through data and Strava does this so well, right?

186
00:14:03,680 --> 00:14:13,040
You build history, you have all this, you know, not only like you have the data points of each ride, but within the rides, you have like all kinds depending on what kind of sensors you're running.

187
00:14:13,360 --> 00:14:16,640
But even now with just the phone, you get a lot, right?

188
00:14:16,960 --> 00:14:20,880
Just from the GPS and maybe like a watch or something like this.

189
00:14:21,760 --> 00:14:23,520
And so I can see why.

190
00:14:24,560 --> 00:14:31,600
It's interesting that you found that the bottleneck was the user interface and not just the friction of I download the app.

191
00:14:31,920 --> 00:14:34,800
Am I like ready to go for a run right now or a ride?

192
00:14:35,440 --> 00:14:37,040
Which I'm sure has its own challenges.

193
00:14:38,240 --> 00:14:38,320
Yeah.

194
00:14:38,640 --> 00:14:44,960
I mean, that's one of like our activation metric is getting you to upload once and follow one person in your first seven days.

195
00:14:45,680 --> 00:14:49,600
And it's a tough activation metric because we need you to go outside.

196
00:14:49,760 --> 00:14:49,840
Yeah.

197
00:14:50,080 --> 00:14:52,800
And our metric actually shows that nobody wants to do that.

198
00:14:53,360 --> 00:14:53,520
Right.

199
00:14:53,680 --> 00:14:57,040
And we actually have a minimum distance of an activity that we also look at.

200
00:14:57,200 --> 00:14:57,600
Oh, wow.

201
00:14:57,840 --> 00:14:58,880
Because we want to make sure like you were.

202
00:14:59,200 --> 00:15:00,240
So you can always just hit it, right?

203
00:15:00,400 --> 00:15:01,040
And then test it.

204
00:15:01,440 --> 00:15:02,720
And then you're like, okay.

205
00:15:04,240 --> 00:15:12,160
And it's a bit challenging because, you know, when you compare to, like, other companies' activation metrics, they're like, well, you can, you know, you can set your profile up in bed.

206
00:15:12,480 --> 00:15:13,360
You know, you're just lying there.

207
00:15:13,440 --> 00:15:15,040
You're using the app for the first time.

208
00:15:15,200 --> 00:15:19,520
You can make free videos, add a friend, you know, all those things you can do from your, from your seat.

209
00:15:20,560 --> 00:15:22,080
But for us, it's amazing.

210
00:15:22,320 --> 00:15:24,400
Like, our users download the product.

211
00:15:25,120 --> 00:15:27,920
About half of them are ready to go right now and go outside.

212
00:15:28,080 --> 00:15:31,120
It's like a it's like an impulse thing or like, oh, I need I'm gonna run.

213
00:15:31,360 --> 00:15:31,760
I need an app.

214
00:15:32,320 --> 00:15:32,640
Exactly.

215
00:15:32,960 --> 00:15:33,760
And that kind of makes it.

216
00:15:33,840 --> 00:15:37,120
Thanks to the app store for making that even a possibility, right?

217
00:15:37,440 --> 00:15:44,960
The fact that you can be like just in time need for software and then you can find it, uh, that's that's pretty cool, but that's crazy.

218
00:15:45,040 --> 00:15:46,640
It's half, but that's good.

219
00:15:46,880 --> 00:15:47,680
That's great for you, right?

220
00:15:47,920 --> 00:15:53,520
Like you have a you have a user who's like completely prime to buy, like when they when they open the app for the first time.

221
00:15:53,840 --> 00:15:54,160
That's great.

222
00:15:54,880 --> 00:16:12,240
So Jason stepping back to the the growth team, uh, I I still wanted to understand how how you went from, hey, we should probably have a growth team to now five separate multidisciplinary teams with over seventy people.

223
00:16:12,720 --> 00:16:13,840
That's that's, oh wow.

224
00:16:14,240 --> 00:16:14,960
Pretty incredible.

225
00:16:15,760 --> 00:16:17,920
And I would have just never, you know, guessed that.

226
00:16:18,160 --> 00:16:24,880
So I'd love to hear a little bit about that kind of evolution and then what those, those teams and functions look like today at Strava.

227
00:16:25,360 --> 00:16:25,440
Yeah.

228
00:16:26,080 --> 00:16:29,360
So the first team for a while, we were just called the growth team, right?

229
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:33,680
But if you looked at the work we were doing, you would classify it as an activation team.

230
00:16:33,840 --> 00:16:35,200
So new user activation.

231
00:16:35,680 --> 00:16:46,960
And I think for the majority of other companies and organizations that I talk to, that news or kind of optimization tends to be the place where you can get the most wins from early and like a very important place.

232
00:16:47,120 --> 00:16:52,480
And, you know, it's connecting your people that have shown intent to come to your product, you know, with the value of your product.

233
00:16:52,880 --> 00:16:54,560
And if you don't have that, then you're in trouble.

234
00:16:55,360 --> 00:16:59,360
From there, we kind of expanded to news or acquisition.

235
00:17:00,160 --> 00:17:01,840
And so we would like to support paid.

236
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:08,000
We would also do kind of referral loops through activity sharing mainly, but basically UGC sharing.

237
00:17:08,640 --> 00:17:15,840
And so we kind of expanded saying, okay, we want to, we had this insight of we have, you know, the ability to share a link from an account.

238
00:17:15,840 --> 00:17:19,200
from activities that generates several hundred thousand shares a month.

239
00:17:19,440 --> 00:17:22,080
Let's try to bump that number up and then convert on the other side.

240
00:17:22,560 --> 00:17:29,680
And so I think early on we were, because we didn't have a strict definition of what a team, we could bounce around from different areas.

241
00:17:29,920 --> 00:17:36,320
And our head of growth, who is actually the CTO, founder of Strava, he decided to one day, he just came and sat next to me.

242
00:17:36,400 --> 00:17:37,920
He's like, I am now the head of growth.

243
00:17:39,040 --> 00:17:43,200
And he really created the ability for us to try things out in different areas.

244
00:17:43,360 --> 00:17:44,960
And so we're like, okay, social sharing.

245
00:17:45,120 --> 00:17:45,680
Let's try this out.

246
00:17:45,840 --> 00:17:47,040
Oh, it's driving new reg.

247
00:17:47,840 --> 00:17:48,560
Let's work on this.

248
00:17:49,280 --> 00:17:52,080
And so we kind of expanded to like activation and acquisition.

249
00:17:52,720 --> 00:17:58,320
And then while the whole that was happening, we had our business model was subscriptions mainly, right?

250
00:17:58,480 --> 00:18:01,200
We mainly make our money through a subscription model.

251
00:18:02,080 --> 00:18:04,000
And that was considered separate to growth.

252
00:18:04,480 --> 00:18:12,160
But really for a long time that was it wasn't growth focused, it was more built features for subscription.

253
00:18:13,040 --> 00:18:25,200
And then eventually, we kind of that became a little bit more growth focused on like, okay, let's increase the value of our up, you know, the conversion of our upsells and let's look at friction and then kind of start to grow work.

254
00:18:25,360 --> 00:18:40,640
So over time, that kind of shifted into the growth org and became a classic kind of growth monetization team, growth subscriptions team through kind of like because of the success we had in the other areas of the business with the growth team of reducing friction, increasing conversion, expanding value.

255
00:18:41,120 --> 00:18:43,040
And so those are kind of like the three main areas.

256
00:18:43,600 --> 00:18:48,560
The two other areas in growth were comms, like we call it the communications and personalization team.

257
00:18:49,280 --> 00:19:00,000
And that was, again, a way to invest in the notification and email and in app messaging life cycle, knowing that that was an important area that we could improve on.

258
00:19:00,560 --> 00:19:05,200
And a lot of benefit, a lot of inspiration here came from Pinterest and their growth comms team.

259
00:19:05,440 --> 00:19:12,800
And again, like a lot of times, these areas start off as one person trying a bunch of experiments in an area and us being like, wow, that's working.

260
00:19:13,520 --> 00:19:14,480
Let's invest more in here.

261
00:19:14,960 --> 00:19:21,680
And then also while we're doing all this, Strava is still having crazy success, right, and growing and reaching more and more users.

262
00:19:22,080 --> 00:19:29,520
And so naturally, the teams start to get a little bit bigger as you need to build the growth tooling infrastructure to be able to do it.

263
00:19:30,080 --> 00:19:34,960
And so it's not as I kind of tell people who join now, like back in the day, I was an IOS engineer.

264
00:19:35,040 --> 00:19:39,440
I could just basically write a whole bunch of tests without really wondering about the scale of it.

265
00:19:39,760 --> 00:19:44,400
And now we have to think about, oh, is this test I'm going to do is going to scale, do I need tools?

266
00:19:45,360 --> 00:19:47,040
You know, what's going to happen when we launch this?

267
00:19:47,280 --> 00:19:48,640
Like, those kind of things necessity.

268
00:19:48,960 --> 00:19:49,280
How does that?

269
00:19:50,400 --> 00:19:59,040
I mean, part of the, you know, the cell of, you know, you were describing what a good growth engineer looks like and somebody kind of who could be a PM is like very fast.

270
00:19:59,360 --> 00:20:02,560
This sounds very, to me like very early startup person, right?

271
00:20:02,640 --> 00:20:07,600
Like someone who's like rapidly iterating to find a good engineer to hire in your first like five or ten, right?

272
00:20:07,840 --> 00:20:11,200
Someone who's, who can move independently and very quickly.

273
00:20:12,240 --> 00:20:19,600
But how does that, you know, you go from one little scrappy team of five to seventy people with multiple teams and things like this?

274
00:20:19,920 --> 00:20:26,000
Like, I imagine in the microcosm of a growth organization, you probably still have some of those scaling issues, right?

275
00:20:26,240 --> 00:20:29,600
Where, suddenly, like, teams are conflicting on tests and stuff like that.

276
00:20:29,760 --> 00:20:32,800
Did you have, was there like a crossing the gap as you spread these?

277
00:20:33,200 --> 00:20:34,880
And then, yeah, and I have a follow up question.

278
00:20:35,040 --> 00:20:36,320
So I'll answer that one first.

279
00:20:37,440 --> 00:20:38,320
Yeah, no, totally.

280
00:20:39,120 --> 00:20:42,320
I mean, I think people ask me, why have I been at Strava for so long?

281
00:20:42,400 --> 00:20:44,560
And I kind of say, every year, it feels like a new job.

282
00:20:44,880 --> 00:20:46,960
So I don't have to go somewhere else for a new experience.

283
00:20:47,040 --> 00:20:51,280
Like, as you're growing, there's also new people coming in and new people, you know, people leaving.

284
00:20:51,520 --> 00:20:57,360
And so there's a little bit of that life cycle of teams that allow you to change as key.

285
00:20:57,760 --> 00:21:04,800
people leave, you can kind of make shifts that, you know, that maybe were just easier when you have someone new coming in to a new role.

286
00:21:05,120 --> 00:21:06,640
But yeah, there's definitely been growing pain.

287
00:21:06,800 --> 00:21:10,880
I mean, I think in terms of like, how much do you know about what the other teams are working on?

288
00:21:11,360 --> 00:21:14,480
How are, you know, product managers interacting, collaborating?

289
00:21:14,880 --> 00:21:17,200
What are the overhead of some of the meetings and stuff we've had?

290
00:21:17,360 --> 00:21:24,560
So instance, for one, one way we're still working through right now is this meeting or this process we call experiments weekly.

291
00:21:25,040 --> 00:21:32,480
So forever since the beginning of Strava, which is on like my journey at Strava and the growth team at Strava, we've had this meeting once a week called experiments weekly.

292
00:21:32,640 --> 00:21:37,840
We represent all the experiments that the growth org team has run and amazing meeting.

293
00:21:37,920 --> 00:21:40,480
And early on, it's, you know, five people and you get to debate.

294
00:21:40,880 --> 00:21:41,440
Why did this happen?

295
00:21:41,600 --> 00:21:42,160
Oh, this happens.

296
00:21:42,320 --> 00:21:42,720
Here's my thing.

297
00:21:42,800 --> 00:21:43,520
Here's the follow-up.

298
00:21:43,600 --> 00:21:45,760
And then, like, you know, within two days, you have another one running.

299
00:21:46,320 --> 00:21:48,560
As you grow, it becomes a bigger meeting, right?

300
00:21:49,040 --> 00:21:54,000
And you have a lot of people like me being bureaucrats now, um, as I kind of call myself in the meeting.

301
00:21:54,160 --> 00:22:02,800
And you have someone who's, you know, me eight years ago, new to the role, not really wanting to speak up and be like, oh, I disagree with that hypothesis, right?

302
00:22:03,040 --> 00:22:05,440
So then you're like, hmm, okay, what do we do?

303
00:22:05,600 --> 00:22:12,960
And so we got to the point where we had like seventy people on this invite last year and the only people that were speaking were, you know, leads of leads kind of thing, right?

304
00:22:13,120 --> 00:22:20,000
So I, you know, oversee growth, the old growth teams, you know, we have a head of analytics who supports all of growth, the other growth teams.

305
00:22:20,160 --> 00:22:21,920
And like we were talking but no one else was.

306
00:22:22,480 --> 00:22:31,280
And so we started seeing one of the growth teams had started the exact same meeting on their own and they were having so much success with brainstorming and everyone being together and in the weeds.

307
00:22:31,840 --> 00:22:32,720
So we're like, huh?

308
00:22:32,880 --> 00:22:33,840
Okay, what do we do about this?

309
00:22:34,080 --> 00:22:37,280
So we canceled the big meetings that all the small teams have their own meetings.

310
00:22:37,680 --> 00:22:39,440
And that's been great, apart from for people like me.

311
00:22:39,520 --> 00:22:41,280
And I'm like, what's the bigger learning?

312
00:22:41,360 --> 00:22:41,920
How are we talking?

313
00:22:42,080 --> 00:22:45,200
You know, and so we're still trying to figure this out and like, what's the right process?

314
00:22:45,520 --> 00:22:46,160
Meetings are tough.

315
00:22:46,240 --> 00:22:47,120
I now live in Ireland.

316
00:22:47,440 --> 00:22:48,400
So time zones are tough.

317
00:22:49,760 --> 00:22:57,520
And yeah, it's tough, you know, it's how do you how do you create that, that narrative or figure out that narrative of learning that matters?

318
00:22:57,600 --> 00:23:00,160
And yeah, you know, especially on a growth team, right?

319
00:23:00,320 --> 00:23:05,360
Where it's by definition, it's rapidity and testing and experimentation.

320
00:23:05,920 --> 00:23:09,600
And I think there's benefit, and I don't think everyone agrees with me on this, which is good.

321
00:23:10,080 --> 00:23:18,000
But I think there's benefit to a team being laser focused on a specific set of areas and almost not wondering about the world around them and getting to move fast.

322
00:23:18,480 --> 00:23:20,560
But then you need to have someone who's pulling out those threads.

323
00:23:20,880 --> 00:23:27,200
Yeah, because I imagine there's collisions and things sometimes where two teams are testing in different directions that don't work well and stuff like this.

324
00:23:27,440 --> 00:23:32,480
Or yeah, and it's like, hey, this learning here and this learning here together, that's a pretty huge insight, right?

325
00:23:33,280 --> 00:23:34,400
And like, how did you do that?

326
00:23:34,640 --> 00:23:36,960
And that's something that we haven't figured out fully.

327
00:23:37,600 --> 00:23:39,920
I see it as part, part of my job, I get to do that.

328
00:23:40,080 --> 00:23:46,640
So I've, I fly a lot for work and I pull up all the experiments that we've run recently before my flights.

329
00:23:46,800 --> 00:23:49,120
And so on my flights, I'm just like reading through experiments.

330
00:23:49,280 --> 00:23:54,320
And so the poor, poor people in my team, you know, I land and I just, I know, tons of Slack.

331
00:23:54,400 --> 00:23:57,200
I pre-write Slack and I just, I'm hammering them with like, why this?

332
00:23:57,360 --> 00:23:58,160
I disagree with this learning.

333
00:23:58,160 --> 00:23:58,640
What about this?

334
00:23:58,720 --> 00:23:59,040
This is cool.

335
00:23:59,120 --> 00:24:00,320
Why are we, you know, let's talk about this.

336
00:24:00,400 --> 00:24:00,960
Let's celebrate this.

337
00:24:01,040 --> 00:24:04,400
Like, did you know this is one of of the best, like, best results I've ever seen?

338
00:24:04,720 --> 00:24:08,000
Like, why wasn't you running around screaming about this test?

339
00:24:08,240 --> 00:24:08,880
This was awesome.

340
00:24:09,040 --> 00:24:10,560
And you know, it's getting overwhelmed.

341
00:24:10,640 --> 00:24:13,440
So I think that's one kind of, like, good problem to have.

342
00:24:13,840 --> 00:24:14,000
Sure.

343
00:24:14,320 --> 00:24:23,120
I would say another one is in the tools that we've built for a while, we didn't have people doing feature education, teams doing feature education.

344
00:24:23,520 --> 00:24:28,640
Teams were very much focused on like the core user, the engaged user with their features.

345
00:24:28,880 --> 00:24:35,280
And so the new user experience or the winback experience resurrection, if you call them those instead, are similar, right?

346
00:24:35,440 --> 00:24:38,480
It's like someone's coming back to teach them about how to use this thing.

347
00:24:39,200 --> 00:24:44,480
And so we and I write about this in my blog, which you can you can read later.

348
00:24:45,200 --> 00:24:49,680
We built the service for this growth team, for this growth product we had to do.

349
00:24:49,760 --> 00:24:50,720
We were working on the feed.

350
00:24:50,960 --> 00:24:53,200
So we built the service to allow us to do feature education.

351
00:24:53,680 --> 00:24:57,840
And for a few years, but probably two years, no one really used it apart from growth team.

352
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:02,480
So we would add coach marks and feature education popups and tool tips and kind of do things around the product.

353
00:25:02,720 --> 00:25:03,120
And it was great.

354
00:25:03,440 --> 00:25:09,840
And then teams, for many, you know, cultural reasons, different product managers, different focuses, everyone started being like, this is important.

355
00:25:10,080 --> 00:25:12,320
We're going to do more feature education.

356
00:25:12,800 --> 00:25:16,080
And now it's funny because I was just in a like a road map review.

357
00:25:16,560 --> 00:25:28,880
and one of the teams, one of the active, our activation team, their test is to remove all of the feature education in a test, you know, we call those negative tests to see, you know, what is the overall impact of something?

358
00:25:29,200 --> 00:25:30,160
Do we need to take a step back?

359
00:25:30,480 --> 00:25:34,800
And it was so wild to me because I was like, I tried for so long to get people to do feature education.

360
00:25:35,280 --> 00:25:38,400
Now we have too much feature education and we need to take a step back.

361
00:25:38,640 --> 00:25:49,760
I mean, this is a, this is a classic, like, not say a late stage, but you always hear this joke about Facebook and things like this about how, you know, like, um, there are certain real estate and apps that become super overcrowded, right?

362
00:25:49,840 --> 00:25:52,240
And I especially imagine when you have federation is good.

363
00:25:52,880 --> 00:25:59,680
But then at some point, you know, you talk about they don't see the small teams might not see the externalities of the competition they're having.

364
00:25:59,760 --> 00:26:00,240
And it's good.

365
00:26:00,320 --> 00:26:02,480
I mean, it's great that you guys have a culture where you can test that.

366
00:26:02,720 --> 00:26:09,280
I was going to ask, and this is kind of related, but my follow-on question was, are there any just normal product teams left at Strava?

367
00:26:09,840 --> 00:26:14,000
Is it just like all because I'm like thinking, I'm like, yeah, you just kind of named all the parts of your app.

368
00:26:14,240 --> 00:26:18,400
I don't know what there's left to do, which, which actually, if that's true, is fascinating.

369
00:26:18,560 --> 00:26:20,080
Just say, like, yeah, we don't have product teams.

370
00:26:20,160 --> 00:26:24,320
We just have growth teams that focus on different parts, which probably for a startup is what you should mostly be doing.

371
00:26:24,640 --> 00:26:24,720
So.

372
00:26:25,360 --> 00:26:25,520
Yeah.

373
00:26:25,920 --> 00:26:26,320
Uh, no.

374
00:26:26,560 --> 00:26:27,600
And it's good that we don't.

375
00:26:27,680 --> 00:26:34,320
You know, there's always that core value that you need to be investing in right and that's I don't think that's the growth team's necessarily job.

376
00:26:34,640 --> 00:26:41,040
I think we can sometimes find ways to expand core value or extract more value, but I don't think it's necessarily expanding it.

377
00:26:41,440 --> 00:26:48,640
So we have like the activities team that's focusing on like the core now in Strava, which is the activity and all the features and analysis around that.

378
00:26:49,280 --> 00:26:54,080
We have the geo team, which is focusing on RAF recommendations, exploring the world around you.

379
00:26:55,360 --> 00:27:01,920
Mapping a big acquisition of Snapmap, which is a great company that has amazing software and amazing user experiences.

380
00:27:02,240 --> 00:27:11,600
And then we have another team that's focused on more of the social experiences, which right now is a lot of around just, yeah, the social experiences, clubs, challenges of that kind of things.

381
00:27:11,840 --> 00:27:19,600
And so a lot of like how we're thinking about this, and this is definitely not something we're perfect at, but a lot of the teams think of themselves as platforms.

382
00:27:20,080 --> 00:27:29,120
Like we're creating the ability to use the activity, the club, the follow, the route in many places in the product, right?

383
00:27:29,360 --> 00:27:33,040
We want to be able to surface this through an email, through a push notification, through the feed.

384
00:27:33,280 --> 00:27:36,720
We want to be able to really modularize our, our, our, our product.

385
00:27:37,760 --> 00:27:44,160
And so a lot of the work we do is just to kind of view ourselves and view teams through themselves as platform creative products.

386
00:27:44,160 --> 00:27:48,080
platform, create a platform and then use that platform to optimize and deliver a user experience.

387
00:27:48,880 --> 00:27:52,800
We kind of went down this rabbit hole talking about feature education.

388
00:27:53,760 --> 00:27:55,520
What does that look like tactically?

389
00:27:55,840 --> 00:28:01,440
Like little pop-ups in the app, like what does the feature education discipline look like?

390
00:28:01,680 --> 00:28:10,160
Because this is something I think from onboarding to all sorts of parts of the app, I think a lot of developers miss some of these kind of fundamentals.

391
00:28:10,800 --> 00:28:10,880
Yeah.

392
00:28:11,520 --> 00:28:22,160
And one of the blessings and curses of Strava in a way is that everyone, you know, most employees use the product every day, which is great, but you're not using the product from the perspective of a new user, right?

393
00:28:22,400 --> 00:28:31,120
Or a way back, or someone who uses it once a week, twice a week, like an average person, you know, not everyone is running and cycling every day, um, et cetera.

394
00:28:31,280 --> 00:28:33,360
And so that kind of empathy is tough.

395
00:28:33,600 --> 00:28:35,760
Um, we have some things that we've built to help with that.

396
00:28:35,920 --> 00:28:41,600
Like we have a switch that you can just make your whole profile free and most of our subscriptions teams just sit in that mode.

397
00:28:42,000 --> 00:28:53,200
So for feature education from us, it's tool tips, kind of coach marks, it's in app, you know, little units of saying like this is how to work, it's push notifications, it's emails, it's all so many different channels.

398
00:28:53,440 --> 00:28:56,480
And of course, the next question you might ask is how do you manage all those channels?

399
00:28:56,560 --> 00:28:58,400
And the question is, we don't do that very very well.

400
00:28:59,200 --> 00:29:02,400
You know, we just hired a new Chief Design Officer.

401
00:29:02,640 --> 00:29:03,280
She's fantastic.

402
00:29:03,360 --> 00:29:04,480
Her name is Anita from Twitter.

403
00:29:04,720 --> 00:29:08,640
And she's asking all these questions about, can I have the user mapping and can I just see the user journey?

404
00:29:09,120 --> 00:29:11,120
And we're like, we'd love to see that too.

405
00:29:12,960 --> 00:29:21,760
And, you know, I think here, I'm like, you know, I I probably think a lot of people feel relieved when they hear that there's always chaos, right?

406
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:24,240
There's always a little bit of chaos with it internally.

407
00:29:24,480 --> 00:29:27,760
And it's easy for people to look at Strava and be like, oh, they have their stuff together, right?

408
00:29:27,920 --> 00:29:29,040
Clearly, they know what they're doing.

409
00:29:29,120 --> 00:29:33,840
And it's like internally, it's like, man, like sometimes we don't even know, you know, exactly what every user is seeing at any time.

410
00:29:34,160 --> 00:29:35,360
Observability is hard.

411
00:29:35,920 --> 00:29:36,560
That's a hard problem.

412
00:29:37,040 --> 00:29:44,240
And I think I appreciate the people around me being willing to still move forward and still make decisions even if we don't have that perfect observability.

413
00:29:44,400 --> 00:29:46,720
And, you know, it's it's something we're trying to solve for.

414
00:29:47,200 --> 00:29:50,240
But, and this is, you know, depends on your entegram or your personality.

415
00:29:50,400 --> 00:29:53,280
I, I like to live in a world of, like, managed chaos.

416
00:29:53,920 --> 00:30:05,200
And it's this, this management philosophy by Eric Schmidt, where it's like, let people do their job and like, maintain just enough order in the chaos so that you can still make decisions, but be okay with chaos.

417
00:30:05,520 --> 00:30:05,600
Yeah.

418
00:30:05,840 --> 00:30:13,280
I mean, if you spend as a leader or, you know, a team leader or whatever, your whole time, like, trying to make sure every everyone's coloring within the lines.

419
00:30:13,520 --> 00:30:19,760
Like you're just going to end up spending your whole time trying to cut off downside and and miss a whole bunch of upside opportunity, right?

420
00:30:20,000 --> 00:30:23,760
Versus, like you were saying, I'm sure this has happened many times over the last eight years.

421
00:30:24,000 --> 00:30:27,680
Like someone ships something that does something they don't expect is bad.

422
00:30:28,560 --> 00:30:31,040
My experience is you often catch it pretty quick, right?

423
00:30:31,360 --> 00:30:34,480
Like if it's that bad, customers will tell you, you'll find it.

424
00:30:34,720 --> 00:30:36,000
You can roll it back really quickly.

425
00:30:36,240 --> 00:30:43,360
Ideally, you have that infrastructure in place where you can roll it back really quickly and a culture and you've built some tools that allow you to roll it back really quickly.

426
00:30:43,600 --> 00:30:48,560
But you can think of it like, you know, as a microcosm of how the greater economy organizes itself, right?

427
00:30:49,120 --> 00:30:52,560
which is through like chaos and competition and stuff like this.

428
00:30:52,960 --> 00:31:16,960
It's not similar to how, you know, when you guys reach a scale, you know, obviously you guys have, you know, seventy people just on the growth side, you can do this, but if you don't preserve that, you end up with, yeah, the opposite, which is waterfall top down bureaucracy, slow behemoth, never innovating, you know, at the cost of never upsetting anyone, which I just don't think works, you know, works maybe if you're Oracle, doesn't work if you're Strava.

429
00:31:17,600 --> 00:31:17,760
Right.

430
00:31:18,080 --> 00:31:20,240
And I, yeah, I mean, that's so, that's so key.

431
00:31:20,720 --> 00:31:24,320
And also something I have to learn and remind myself every day.

432
00:31:24,400 --> 00:31:26,560
I, you know, I was, I was a bureaucrat.

433
00:31:26,640 --> 00:31:27,360
You're like, what is it?

434
00:31:27,440 --> 00:31:28,880
What's a bureaucrat's job in this world?

435
00:31:29,360 --> 00:31:33,920
And it's funny because I remember being the guy that was just running tons of tests.

436
00:31:34,000 --> 00:31:40,640
Like I was looking back at the spreadsheet of tests I ran, you know, almost eight years ago now, just, you know, from that year, like, what did I run?

437
00:31:40,800 --> 00:31:41,280
What do we do?

438
00:31:41,760 --> 00:31:45,440
And just remembering how fun it was just like churn out test after test after test.

439
00:31:45,840 --> 00:31:54,160
And so many of the tests were just my ideas and my ideas worked and the bureaucrat, you know, maybe the ideas didn't work because they weren't as close to the problem or thinking about it night and day.

440
00:31:54,480 --> 00:32:05,440
And now I have to think to myself, like, I have all this context and I have all these opinions, but they don't necessarily like, I'm so far away from it now that I need to trust the teams to do their job.

441
00:32:05,920 --> 00:32:09,600
Even when it looks like chaos, I need to remember, like, what amount is okay?

442
00:32:10,240 --> 00:32:14,160
Am I just making people's lives harder by the questions I'm asking?

443
00:32:14,320 --> 00:32:17,120
How do I empower them to do their job really well?

444
00:32:17,440 --> 00:32:22,320
Because the people on these teams become the experts and they become, you know, they want to do their job really well.

445
00:32:22,560 --> 00:32:23,920
They want to see business impact.

446
00:32:24,960 --> 00:32:29,840
And my job is to kind of, you know, help them do their job really well versus tell them what to do.

447
00:32:30,000 --> 00:32:38,640
And that's why I mean, engineers thinking like product managers, some of our best wins have come from engineers six months out of college, a year out of college, who was like, I want to try this.

448
00:32:38,960 --> 00:32:40,720
And we actually did we used to do this thing.

449
00:32:40,880 --> 00:32:52,960
We haven't done as much recently, but we used to especially when we were in person, all in person, we used to do this thing called experiments day, where like once a quarter there was a day where the team was allowed to run whatever experiments they wanted.

450
00:32:53,280 --> 00:32:59,760
There were some rules like, don't take away a subscription upsell and don't crash the app kind of thing, and it has to be only ten percent of users.

451
00:33:00,080 --> 00:33:06,160
But there was no, there was no necessary buy in needed from an engineering manager, a product manager.

452
00:33:06,880 --> 00:33:12,400
And of course, the risk averse folks would see that as, oh, they're just going to chip whatever they want.

453
00:33:12,560 --> 00:33:13,360
It's going to be hacky.

454
00:33:13,440 --> 00:33:14,080
It's going to be bad.

455
00:33:14,160 --> 00:33:15,120
They're not going to collaborate.

456
00:33:15,280 --> 00:33:17,920
You know, we just took a step back and the team still worked together.

457
00:33:18,080 --> 00:33:22,560
Like they all worked, literally every single person still worked together, design, analytics, engineering.

458
00:33:22,960 --> 00:33:33,280
They came out with the ideas and a lot of the ideas were things that we had not prioritized because the data said probably not a lot of juice in that squeeze.

459
00:33:33,680 --> 00:33:38,160
So an instance here, and shout out to Annie who worked on the growth team for a long time at Strava.

460
00:33:38,800 --> 00:33:44,080
She really wanted to run a test where if someone followed you and you saw their profile anywhere, it said follow back.

461
00:33:44,960 --> 00:33:48,800
And the data said the majority of relationships in Strava were reciprocal.

462
00:33:49,120 --> 00:33:49,840
So what's the point?

463
00:33:50,400 --> 00:33:52,880
But she was like, okay, I'm going to run this on experiments day.

464
00:33:53,120 --> 00:33:59,840
And she ran it and resulted in the most, the biggest increase in follow rate that we've ever, that we've ever seen.

465
00:34:00,640 --> 00:34:04,400
And again, it was small, reduced friction, the simple insight, nothing mindful.

466
00:34:04,960 --> 00:34:06,160
Do you remember what the myth was on that?

467
00:34:06,320 --> 00:34:09,920
Like, I mean, because I was just, you know, he shows people a button, they're going to push it, right?

468
00:34:10,160 --> 00:34:13,600
So, you, I mean, might be long time gone now.

469
00:34:13,920 --> 00:34:15,920
But I, I mean, I see that can happen all the time.

470
00:34:16,080 --> 00:34:23,440
You just, organizationally, you, I always like to say a whisper becomes a myth, becomes a legend, becomes like just the truth, right?

471
00:34:23,760 --> 00:34:24,480
This happens all the time.

472
00:34:24,640 --> 00:34:30,480
And like, you can see how someone ran a half experiment five years ago and it failed, and then it just becomes knowledge.

473
00:34:31,120 --> 00:34:34,320
Like, yeah, I don't know if if if you're having to remember for that case.

474
00:34:35,520 --> 00:34:36,640
I don't remember exactly.

475
00:34:36,800 --> 00:34:45,680
I, I, you know, it's one of those things that was so simple and so low cost to do that you think of yourself like, why did we ever, why did we even talk about that?

476
00:34:45,840 --> 00:34:53,040
I think that's the thing that probably I try to do the most now is where are we talking about something more than it would cost to just build it?

477
00:34:53,280 --> 00:34:54,400
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

478
00:34:54,960 --> 00:35:09,200
And I think, you know, that's ultimately probably what my philosophy, I think my role is at Strava is to make it, make it easy enough to test any and every idea, rather than us having to self select here of the five designs you have as a designs you have as a designer, here's the one you think is the most effective.

479
00:35:09,440 --> 00:35:13,440
Just reduce debate by making debate the more energy consuming thing.

480
00:35:13,680 --> 00:35:14,560
Like just testing.

481
00:35:14,640 --> 00:35:15,920
I think it's more inclusive, right?

482
00:35:16,080 --> 00:35:27,200
I think because you are, if you move fast and you're able to test a lot of things in a very scientific way, you remove the bias of someone, you know, someone, the loudest voice in the room, right?

483
00:35:27,520 --> 00:35:28,720
Becoming the person who, yeah.

484
00:35:29,200 --> 00:35:29,360
Right.

485
00:35:29,440 --> 00:35:33,120
And you instead say, we want everyone's ideas and we're going to test every idea possible.

486
00:35:33,360 --> 00:35:34,640
And obviously there's a limit to that.

487
00:35:34,720 --> 00:35:38,160
But we would like, we want to test things and we want to create safe spaces to do that.

488
00:35:38,640 --> 00:35:40,720
And some amazing stuff comes out of that.

489
00:35:41,520 --> 00:35:43,920
Do you guys, and I imagine the answer is going to be yes.

490
00:35:43,920 --> 00:35:46,080
Yes, in some form, but did you design?

491
00:35:47,200 --> 00:36:00,480
I can remember looking at these old, like, user models from Twitter back in the when they're really growing a lot and stuff where you have sort of like a, you know, it's maybe not a perfect model, but you have some sort of activation tied to retention tied to all those things.

492
00:36:00,880 --> 00:36:07,840
You have a mathematical model that you sort of run your tests on top of, like some set of core metrics that you think are interrelated in certain ways.

493
00:36:08,160 --> 00:36:08,960
Did you build that first?

494
00:36:09,200 --> 00:36:09,920
Did you arrive at that?

495
00:36:10,080 --> 00:36:10,560
Do you have that?

496
00:36:11,600 --> 00:36:13,280
You know, that's always a work in progress.

497
00:36:14,000 --> 00:36:14,160
Sure.

498
00:36:14,720 --> 00:36:20,880
We early days, the product analyst on the team, Dave, shout out to Dave was.

499
00:36:20,880 --> 00:36:23,840
was basically running the same SQL query over and over for tests.

500
00:36:24,000 --> 00:36:28,720
And we had a very, we were very always, I think we've always been pretty good about a very small subset of metrics to run after.

501
00:36:29,680 --> 00:36:41,280
And again, we got a lot of help from the early growth people at Facebook and other companies and Pinterest who came in and helped us say, like, this is what an activation metric is, this is what an output metric is, teams should have clear metrics, right?

502
00:36:41,520 --> 00:36:42,400
And so we always had that.

503
00:36:42,640 --> 00:36:50,000
And then my teammates Dave built our internal reporting, experiment reporting framework called One Ring.

504
00:36:50,560 --> 00:36:53,520
And it's an ode to Lord of the Rings, One Ring to rule them all.

505
00:36:53,600 --> 00:36:55,360
So it's like one SQL query to rule them all.

506
00:36:55,440 --> 00:36:56,320
And there's always building stuff.

507
00:36:56,480 --> 00:36:58,720
It's always named after Star Wars and Lord of the Rings.ing.

508
00:36:59,120 --> 00:36:59,440
It's great.

509
00:37:00,160 --> 00:37:00,960
It's still called that.

510
00:37:01,040 --> 00:37:03,040
It still has a little ring in the Tableau dashboard.

511
00:37:03,280 --> 00:37:05,440
And that allowed us that became like the source of truth.

512
00:37:05,680 --> 00:37:09,600
Every experiment, for the most part, obviously, exceptions, has metrics in there.

513
00:37:09,760 --> 00:37:19,360
And now we have, I think, so many metrics that our data engineering team, it's like, we got to stop like these, you know, the jobs to run the experimental analyses overnight are taking forever.

514
00:37:19,840 --> 00:37:26,160
But for the most part, we've been pretty static in terms of like measured outcomes, not modeled outcomes.

515
00:37:26,720 --> 00:37:30,560
We have now we're now trying to do a better job.

516
00:37:30,640 --> 00:37:37,360
And we've been doing, we've done this all of last year of when you move a certain output metric, typically for us, it's week to retention in many cases.

517
00:37:37,920 --> 00:37:43,120
What is the impact on wow over the course of a whole year or whole twelve months?

518
00:37:43,280 --> 00:37:52,960
And so there's like decay built in, is modeling built into, you know, if we increase week to retention for a winback today, what does that actually mean in the course of twelve months?

519
00:37:53,040 --> 00:37:55,840
How many more wow are we going to see in the product?

520
00:37:56,080 --> 00:38:01,520
So you're actually looking in some ways at like leverage of certain metrics versus like they're just face values.

521
00:38:02,320 --> 00:38:02,400
Yeah.

522
00:38:02,880 --> 00:38:09,840
And we're trying, like we have a long, I think, a long way to go to keep doing that with ideally our error amplify as well, right?

523
00:38:10,080 --> 00:38:11,600
When you, when you start to model out.

524
00:38:12,080 --> 00:38:12,160
Yeah.

525
00:38:12,240 --> 00:38:20,560
Yeah, I always get a little nervous on too much reliance on modeling because to create the model you build it on data that you had.

526
00:38:20,800 --> 00:38:26,640
And after a certain while, the data you have was generated by the model you created.

527
00:38:26,720 --> 00:38:33,200
So there's definitely a balance and I think things like hold out groups, long term hold outs can help you with the source of truth.

528
00:38:33,680 --> 00:38:34,800
Yeah, and being simple, right?

529
00:38:35,120 --> 00:38:35,600
Remaining simple.

530
00:38:35,840 --> 00:38:48,560
And I think when you talk to think about the folks listening to this podcast who probably don't have seventy full FTEs sitting around ready to put onto a growth organization, it's like in the early days, like define a few really core key ones.

531
00:38:49,280 --> 00:38:50,320
And don't go test crazy.

532
00:38:50,480 --> 00:39:08,320
I think I've made the mistake in the past during experiment design of wanting to design measurements as close to the experiment as possible when I think that's, you know, be like, okay, well, we're going to change this button, let's specifically change the measure this convergent rate of the button and that's the only thing we can really attribute.

533
00:39:08,720 --> 00:39:15,040
And maybe there's some value in that, but really what you should probably be tracking is how does this affect the overall output?

534
00:39:15,280 --> 00:39:18,960
And if it's not measurable, then it probably doesn't matter.

535
00:39:20,800 --> 00:39:38,240
One of the things I wanted to dive into before we have to wrap up is a couple of tacticalal things, and one of them is that we just haven't talked about this enough on the podcast, and I know you've thought a lot about this on the growth team at Strava, but the App Store is such a black box.

536
00:39:38,800 --> 00:39:59,120
How do you think about that inside Strava from a, and thankfully the App Store has evolved now with custom product pages and stuff like that, but you have to send this person who got a referral, not to something you have control of, but to the black box at the App Store with screenshots and a title and icon and all this.

537
00:40:00,000 --> 00:40:05,920
How do you think about the App Store black box as part of your kind of growth journey?

538
00:40:06,960 --> 00:40:07,760
It's a great question.

539
00:40:08,160 --> 00:40:09,520
Definitely not my foretaste.

540
00:40:10,000 --> 00:40:14,240
I'll answer as much as I can to represent the growth marketing folks in my team well.

541
00:40:14,720 --> 00:40:17,680
You know, there's always optimization, App Store optimization, right?

542
00:40:17,920 --> 00:40:18,720
Classic ASO.

543
00:40:19,840 --> 00:40:23,520
If you've written ASO in articles and still don't know what it means, it's App Store optimization.

544
00:40:23,680 --> 00:40:25,120
I feel like there's a lot of that in growth, right?

545
00:40:25,280 --> 00:40:29,040
There's like a lot of acronyms that get never defined.

546
00:40:30,160 --> 00:40:30,640
It's just like that.

547
00:40:31,600 --> 00:40:35,360
It's changing your App Store photos and images and testing that.

548
00:40:35,440 --> 00:40:36,960
Now that you can actually test, right?

549
00:40:37,360 --> 00:40:39,920
For most time, you couldn't test that.

550
00:40:41,360 --> 00:40:42,560
functionality on the App Store.

551
00:40:42,720 --> 00:40:46,160
And so we tended not to really change it too much because it was hard to attribute.

552
00:40:46,480 --> 00:40:50,320
And is it worth spending time on things that you can't measure?

553
00:40:50,880 --> 00:40:52,480
So now you can, so that's a little better.

554
00:40:52,720 --> 00:40:54,640
The problem there is novelty effect, right?

555
00:40:54,880 --> 00:41:00,560
Whenever you change something, as you said, like with a button earlier, like it can, you know, works the first time, but what about in two months?

556
00:41:01,360 --> 00:41:02,080
It's hard to do that.

557
00:41:02,880 --> 00:41:17,040
I think we've we've tried things in the past where you have, you know, some companies can provide services where you create a fake App Store facade and then you optimize that a bunch and it's really just a web page and you just optimize that a bunch of things and then you take the win of that there.

558
00:41:17,440 --> 00:41:19,120
It's a lot of it's a lot of work.

559
00:41:19,760 --> 00:41:26,640
And, you know, so I think what we've really stuck with is like app store ads, making it very clear what you're, what you're doing on Strava videos.

560
00:41:27,200 --> 00:41:28,240
Videos really helped us.

561
00:41:28,640 --> 00:41:29,600
And then app reviews.

562
00:41:29,840 --> 00:41:37,280
Apple really takes into account how many five star ratings you have in terms of how well they'll rank you.

563
00:41:37,840 --> 00:41:40,000
And so we've really two things.

564
00:41:40,400 --> 00:41:41,600
One, try to have a great app.

565
00:41:42,000 --> 00:41:51,680
So when it comes to like foundational metrics, you know, we always go for four nines, four nines of crash free sessions, four nines of availability from our services, and that helps a lot.

566
00:41:52,160 --> 00:41:55,040
And then also prompting our users to rate the app.

567
00:41:55,520 --> 00:42:02,480
Apple has nice functionality and Google has nice functionality to tell you, hey, you're using the product, why don't you rate us in the App Store?

568
00:42:02,640 --> 00:42:06,880
So we've tried to do things like that, kind of to be good citizens around our App Store presence.

569
00:42:07,120 --> 00:42:19,840
I don't necessarily know if I, again, with Strava being a big organic growth engine, I don't know how much I would give advice to other people based on that because we, people are often searching, you know, just the brand it searches for.

570
00:42:20,160 --> 00:42:27,200
If you're a Strava brand and you have, yeah, channels that are not dependent on it, that's the best App Store optimization advice you can give is like, don't depend on it, right?

571
00:42:27,840 --> 00:42:30,240
Just make it a small multiplier into what you're doing.

572
00:42:30,880 --> 00:42:33,920
I'm sure you guys get some amount of like organic discovery and features.

573
00:42:34,240 --> 00:42:40,880
Have you been successful with, I'm sure you have, like over the years with partnering with Apple and just working the App Store as a growth channel?

574
00:42:41,440 --> 00:42:49,760
We definitely benefit from having features with it with Strava, uh, I was sorry, with Apple, um, in different, especially different countries.

575
00:42:49,920 --> 00:42:53,440
So we often get, hey, we're going to do a feature for you in, you know, Germany.

576
00:42:53,600 --> 00:42:55,120
Can you provide us special assets?

577
00:42:55,920 --> 00:43:04,880
The one interesting thing, I think you can actually learn a little bit from your logged out pages, both on web and mobile, in terms of just what we call Strava the front doors.

578
00:43:05,200 --> 00:43:07,600
So before you've entered the Strava ecosystem, what works?

579
00:43:08,160 --> 00:43:14,800
One of the early tests we did on the activation team was changing the photos that you would see kind of the welcome experience before you signed up.

580
00:43:15,600 --> 00:43:19,280
And we discovered that back then Strava was very much cycling or running.

581
00:43:19,360 --> 00:43:22,080
So we used to ask you, are you a cyclist or are you a runner?

582
00:43:22,800 --> 00:43:23,680
We don't do that anymore.

583
00:43:25,440 --> 00:43:28,720
But that was really interesting because we ran tests where we had for the most part cyclists.

584
00:43:28,800 --> 00:43:30,240
We showed a lot of cycling photos.

585
00:43:30,560 --> 00:43:32,560
And we tested cycling versus running photos.

586
00:43:32,720 --> 00:43:38,480
And we discovered that runners were put off by photos when the splash screen was a cyclist.

587
00:43:38,880 --> 00:43:41,760
Cyclists didn't really care about seeing a runner.

588
00:43:42,480 --> 00:43:46,640
And then obviously men didn't mind seeing men or women and women prefer to see women.

589
00:43:47,120 --> 00:43:51,360
And so that was a very interesting kind of realization of like, we're trying to expand who we serve.

590
00:43:51,440 --> 00:43:52,400
We're trying to serve runners.

591
00:43:52,560 --> 00:43:55,600
We're trying to expand the brand around Strava, not just be cycling.

592
00:43:56,240 --> 00:43:59,440
Maybe we shouldn't lead with cycling photos, um, everywhere.

593
00:43:59,760 --> 00:44:08,480
And so, you know, simple things that you can test either on your website, um, or on your mobile app that then you take those insights into the black box of, of app stores.

594
00:44:09,200 --> 00:44:13,440
And for better for worse, hope that those learnings persist.

595
00:44:13,680 --> 00:44:13,760
Yeah.

